@meta
id: 11-16
title: Scenario — the payout export
chapter: 11
type: scenario
skills: dot-repeat, dot-design, cgn, amp-repeat-sub
par: 104
minutes: 14
vscode: three tabs, one replace-all you would have to check by hand
open: payout.provider.ts payout.constants.ts payout.test.ts

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    PAY-233  The payouts importer was rewritten over the weekend. Three
             things in our code no longer match what it accepts, and the
             batch run is at 17:00.

  1. payout.provider.ts — the importer calls the column `customerId`.
     We call it `custId` everywhere in this file. Rename all six.

  2. payout.constants.ts — the batch string is pipe-separated and the
     importer wants commas. While you are in there, the file came out of
     a converter with no semicolons on any of its four lines. Add them.

  3. payout.test.ts — the ledger is in euros now, so the five assertions
     expect `'EUR'`. The comments on those lines are a record of what the
     refunds were raised in and do not change. Neither does anything else
     in the file.

Everything here is a repeat of something you did earlier in the chapter.
The judgement being tested is which repeat, and noticing that the third
file is the one where the obvious tool is the wrong one.

Write all three and quit in one go when you are done.

@keys
| `:w` then `:bn` | write this file, move to the next one |
| `:ls` | which files are open |
| `*` `cgn` `.` | rename every match of the word under the cursor |
| `f|` `r,` then `;.` | walk a line replacing a separator |
| `A` `;` `Esc` then `j.` | a semicolon on the end of every line |
| `:s/x/y/` then `&` | a substitute, then the same substitute here |
| `:wqa` | write every file and quit. `:wq` will refuse — there are more files |

@hints
Do the files in the order they are open and finish each one before you
move. Vim will not let you leave a modified file behind without a `:w`.
--
The rename is `*` and `cgn` and then dot until the matches run out. The
constants file is two separate repeats: one that walks along a line and
one that walks down the file.
--
In the test file the thing you are matching has quotes around it, and the
same three letters appear in the comment on every line. That rules out
searching for the word — state the pattern once with a substitute and
repeat it down the file with `&`.
--
`:wqa` at the end, not `:wq`. With three files open, `:wq` writes the
first one and then tells you there are more files to edit.

@solution
**payout.provider.ts** — `jw` onto the first `custId`, then `*`, then
`cgncustomerId` and `Esc`, then `.` five times. `:w` and `:bn`.

**payout.constants.ts** — `f|r,` and then `;.` five times takes the
separators. Then `A;` and `Esc` on the first line and `j.` three times
for the rest. `:w` and `:bn`.

**payout.test.ts** — `2j` down to the first assertion,
`:s/'GBP'/'EUR'/` and Enter, then `j&` four times. `:wqa`.

Three files, three repeats, and the only thing you typed more than once
was `j`.

The third file is the decision. `*` on `GBP` would have matched the
comments too — you would have been pressing `n` and `.` alternately down
ten matches and getting it wrong on one of them. The quotes are the part
of the pattern that separates the code from the prose, and only a
substitute can say them. Recognising which of the two repeats fits the
pattern in front of you is the actual skill this chapter has been
teaching; the keys took ten minutes to learn on day one.

One habit to take into chapter 12: every one of these started by naming
the thing rather than by finding it on screen. `*` names a word, `f|`
names a character, `:s` names a pattern. Once it is named, repeating is
free.

@start payout.provider.ts
export type PayoutArgs = {
  custId: string;
  amountPence: number;
};

export async function createPayout(args: PayoutArgs) {
  const customer = await customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id: args.custId } });

  if (!customer) {
    throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
  }

  logger.info('payout requested', { custId: args.custId });

  return ledger.credit({ custId: args.custId, amount: args.amountPence });
}

@expected payout.provider.ts
export type PayoutArgs = {
  customerId: string;
  amountPence: number;
};

export async function createPayout(args: PayoutArgs) {
  const customer = await customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id: args.customerId } });

  if (!customer) {
    throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
  }

  logger.info('payout requested', { customerId: args.customerId });

  return ledger.credit({ customerId: args.customerId, amount: args.amountPence });
}

